questions.question:
Pull request #911 "auth: store refresh tokens as hashes":

const token = randomBytes(32).toString("base64url");
const tokenHash = createHash("sha256").update(token).digest("hex");
await db.query("INSERT INTO auth.refresh_tokens (id, family_id, user_id, device_id, token_hash, created_at, expires_at) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5, now(), now() + interval '30 days')", [id, familyId, userId, deviceId, tokenHash]);

Review comment: "SHA-256 is fast, so an attacker with the backup can brute-force these hashes. Use bcrypt with cost 12, like we do for passwords."

Statement: "The reviewer is right: with plain SHA-256, an attacker who has the backup can recover working refresh tokens from token_hash in practical time."

